35,2,1,talk,Ryuji,,"The data shows a significant imbalance in room usage, with those nearest the door dominating it. Furthermore, the history of the old fridge suggests that maintenance often falls on a single person. We need a sustainable system.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Jonathan,,"It seems we are lacking a space for genuine connection. I've found that our only unplanned interactions are by the printer, and difficult conversations are happening in the open, which is quite unfortunate for everyone involved.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,May,,"It's a waste of time and money. We're spending nearly 70,000 yen on outside rentals for tiny groups, and most of us are losing nearly half an hour just to get a hot lunch. We need efficiency.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Shunichi,,"The numbers are quite telling. We're bleeding money on external rentals for small groups, and the fit-out costs vary significantly. It seems we're paying for a convenience we don't actually have in-house.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Servas,,"The physical reality is just as telling. Our walls are thin, offering no true privacy, and our desks are becoming dining tables, which brings a certain untidiness to our shared workspace.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Ryuji,,"@Jonathan, your point about the lack of privacy is reinforced by @Servas's finding on the walls. If we don't establish a formal purpose, we will continue to have awkward encounters in public.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Jonathan"",""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Jonathan,,"Exactly. When we lack a sanctuary for private or difficult dialogue, we sacrifice our peace of mind. A space that fosters both connection and privacy would be a great kindness to us all.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,May,,"Efficiency is key, but so is sanity. If we're eating at our desks and leaving crumbs, it's because we have no choice. Why waste time walking or money renting when we have a room right here?","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Shunichi,,It's a classic case of inefficiency. We're paying for external rooms for a handful of people while our own space is a free-for-all. We need a solution that stops the financial leak and the desk-crumbs.,"{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Servas,,"It seems we are all describing the same problem from different angles. Whether it is the cost, the crumbs, or the lack of privacy, we are currently lacking a sanctuary that serves us all with dignity.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Ryuji,,"If we choose a kitchen, we risk repeating the fridge disaster where one person does all the cleaning. A dedicated meeting space or quiet room would be easier to maintain and more equitable.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Jonathan,,"A meeting space could solve the issue of awkward conversations and the cost of outside rentals, provided it remains a place where we can actually speak honestly and privately.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,May,,"@Ryuji, you're always so worried about the cleaning! We can just set a schedule. The real issue is the time we're wasting. A kitchen would save us hours of walking every single week.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Shunichi,,"A schedule is just a piece of paper, May. People ignore them. However, the financial leak from those external rentals is a fact. A meeting space seems the most rational way to stop that bleeding.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Servas,,"We must consider that a meeting space, while rational for the budget, may not solve the noise issue if the walls remain as thin as they are. We need a sanctuary of true peace.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Ryuji,,"Regardless of the purpose, the thin walls mean we must be mindful of noise. However, a meeting space addresses the financial waste and the lack of privacy for difficult talks without the maintenance burden of a kitchen.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Jonathan,,"I agree. A meeting space provides the necessary sanctuary for those difficult conversations and restores a sense of professional dignity to our interactions, while also being more sustainable than a kitchen.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,May,,"I'm not convinced. A meeting space doesn't stop the crumbs or the long walks for food. If we want to stop wasting time and money, a kitchen is the only choice that actually improves our daily lives.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Shunichi,,"May, you're focusing on the lunch walk, but the data shows we're paying for external rooms for tiny groups. A meeting space stops that leak and solves the privacy issue. It's the most logical investment.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Servas,,"We must balance the needs of the body and the spirit. While a kitchen feeds us, a space for quiet reflection and honest dialogue feeds the soul and preserves our collective peace.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
